---
import {Image} from "astro:assets";
import imperial from "../../assets/images/project-notes/imperial.png";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
        title="Helix at Imperial College London"
        description="I adapted InvenioRDM to Imperial’s visual identity, designing and helping implement a distinctive
                    repository interface without changing its established user journeys."
>
    <main>
        <article class="project-note">
            <header class="project-note__header">
                <p class="project-note__eyebrow">Interface design and implementation</p>

                <h1>Bringing Imperial’s identity to a shared repository platform</h1>

                <p class="project-note__summary">
                    I designed and helped implement the interface for Helix, Imperial College
                    London’s institutional data repository, built on the open-source InvenioRDM
                    platform.
                </p>
            </header>

            <figure class="case-study-figure hero">
                <Image src={imperial} format="webp" quality={82}
                       alt=""
                       class=""/>

                <figcaption>
                    The Helix homepage applies Imperial’s visual identity while retaining the
                    established structure and functionality of InvenioRDM.
                </figcaption>
            </figure>

            <section class="project-note__section">
                <h2>Designing within an established platform</h2>

                <p>
                    The project did not require a new repository experience from the ground up.
                    InvenioRDM already provided the principal user journeys and interface
                    components. My task was to make the service feel recognisably part of
                    Imperial without disrupting those established patterns.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I translated Imperial’s visual identity into the repository’s typography,
                    colour, spacing and interface details, and designed a more distinctive
                    homepage that introduces the service and provides clear routes into its
                    content.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="project-note__section">
                <h2>From visual direction to implementation</h2>

                <p>
                    I worked directly with the existing InvenioRDM interface rather than
                    producing an extensive standalone prototype. This allowed design decisions
                    to be tested against real components and technical constraints as the
                    frontend was developed.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I also contributed to the implementation, working within the platform’s
                    templates and styles to carry the visual direction through to the live
                    repository.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="project-note__section">
                <h2>Result</h2>

                <p>
                    Helix retains the familiarity and functionality of InvenioRDM while feeling
                    specific to Imperial College London. The project demonstrates how considered
                    visual design can give a shared open-source platform its own institutional
                    identity without introducing unnecessary changes to its underlying user
                    experience.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <a href="https://helix.imperial.ac.uk/">
                        Visit the Helix data repository
                    </a>
                </p>
            </section>
        </article>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
